{"rq":"RQ1","claim_id":"RQ1-A","finding":"In hierarchical UAV-edge-cloud collaboration topologies, studies separate the physical tier layout from layer-level partition granularity by placing two sequential split points across UAV, edge server, and cloud tiers: R0001 models 10 UAVs, 4 edge servers, and 1 cloud server with two layer split points (p_k, q_k) defining five execution modes, and R0054 models STAR-RIS-assisted UAV, MEC, and cloud tiers with two layer split points (l1, l2) paired with intermediate feature bitwidth selection ({4, 8, 12, 16}).","citations":[{"report_id":"R0001","source":"P08","key":"uav_architecture_role_and_topology:1","physical_page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup"},{"report_id":"R0001","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":2,"section":"II.A Inference Partitioning"},{"report_id":"R0054","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":3,"section":"Section II-B Three-tier DNN inference, Eq. (13) and (17e)"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ1","claim_id":"RQ1-B","finding":"Distinct from sequential layer cuts across cloud/edge tiers, UAV-swarm and edge-UAV topologies adopt sub-layer, spatial feature-map, or DAG partition granularities: R0015 splits individual CNN layers into parallel subtasks across an ad hoc UAV swarm without a ground server, R0044 applies row-wise feature-map backtracking across conv/pool layers over 4 UAV MEC servers with base-station fallback, and R0048 partitions YOLOv3-tiny at the classifier-level branching DAG under centralized or distributed coordination.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0015","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":3,"section":"III.A System Model"},{"report_id":"R0044","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":3,"section":"II.C DNN Partitioning and Offloading"},{"report_id":"R0048","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":7,"section":"V.B Algorithm"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ2","claim_id":"RQ2-A","finding":"Optimization formulations distinguish algorithmic decision variables from fixed experimental settings: R0006 jointly optimizes 2D UAV hovering positions (G) and binary layer assignment (X) via alternating DP and SCA (CVX/MOSEK) to minimize pipeline completion time while fixing altitude, per-UAV compute/memory capacity, transmit power, and coverage radius; R0045 optimizes partitioning, 3D trajectory, association, and migration via SAC-PER plus an inner convex UAV compute solver while fixing max CPU frequencies (1 GHz user / 10 GHz UAV), bandwidths (10 MHz Rician K=10; 15 MHz LoS), transmit powers (0.2 W / 0.5 W), and noise (-85 dBm).","citations":[{"report_id":"R0006","source":"P08","key":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver:1","physical_page":4,"section":"III.B & IV"},{"report_id":"R0006","source":"P08","key":"compute_and_radio:1","physical_page":6,"section":"V-A, UAV specifications and communication/energy parameter bullets"},{"report_id":"R0045","source":"P08","key":"compute_and_radio:1","physical_page":9,"section":"IV.A Simulation Settings"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ2","claim_id":"RQ2-B","finding":"Hybrid DRL and decomposition solvers similarly bound optimization decisions by fixed scenario rules: R0009 optimizes partition point (L*), 2D UAV trajectory (U), compute allocation (b), ground-user transmit power (P), and offloading (alpha) via OPPS, fairness matching, and TD3 while fixing a 400m x 400m area, 2 m/s max speed, and free-space LoS channel; in R0001, PPO and convex subproblems optimize partition mode and compute/bandwidth allocation while edge-server selection defaults to the closest edge server rather than being optimized (P09 D02).","citations":[{"report_id":"R0009","source":"P08","key":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver:1","physical_page":6,"section":"IV.C Trajectory & Power"},{"report_id":"R0009","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":8,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings"},{"report_id":"R0001","source":"P09","key":"D02","physical_page":2,"section":"II.E"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ3","claim_id":"RQ3-A","finding":"Evidence types span profiled software simulations and benchtop hardware testbeds without flight-network validation: R0048 evaluates Python/PyTorch simulations parameterized with measured Raspberry Pi 3B+ and NCS1/NCS2 profiles, whereas R0062 evaluates a physical 4-UAV hardware testbed (3 Jetson Nano + 1 Jetson TX2 with airborne communication modules) that P09 D07 rates PARTIAL because actual flight-network and 3D mobility validation are absent.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0048","source":"P08","key":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces:1","physical_page":9,"section":"VII. NUMERICAL RESULTS"},{"report_id":"R0062","source":"P08","key":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces:1","physical_page":5,"section":"Section EXPERIMENT SETUP"},{"report_id":"R0062","source":"P09","key":"D07","physical_page":5,"section":"Performance Evaluation"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ3","claim_id":"RQ3-B","finding":"Under P09 domain D04 (baseline comparability), 24 of 25 included study units are rated PARTIAL (only R0121 is rated YES); source-located rationales document internal-ablation-only comparator setups without external baselines (R0054's six internal schemes and R0071's no-LSTM MHAPPO ablation) and undisclosed hyperparameter/tuning parity for external DRL baselines (C-PPO, C-DQN, and MADDPG in R0048).","citations":[{"report_id":"R0054","source":"P09","key":"D04","physical_page":4,"section":"§IV Protocol"},{"report_id":"R0071","source":"P09","key":"D04","physical_page":6,"section":"IV. Simulation Results and Discussion"},{"report_id":"R0048","source":"P09","key":"D04","physical_page":10,"section":"VII.A.3 Baselines"}],"frequency":{"n":24,"N":25,"numerator_ids":["R0001","R0002","R0003","R0006","R0009","R0010","R0015","R0019","R0022","R0029","R0044","R0045","R0048","R0054","R0059","R0062","R0063","R0068","R0071","R0073","R0074","R0083","R0089","R0095"],"denominator":"All 25 included study units in the locked HK261 restart (appraisal_table.csv, domain D04 = PARTIAL)"},"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ4","claim_id":"RQ4-A","finding":"Mobility treatment separates static or simplified channel/hovering assumptions from tested dynamic mobility: R0048 assumes hovering at 200 m and defers UAV trajectory and kinematics to future work, whereas R0003 tests trace-driven UAV movement using Mid-Air flight traces and R0045 tests joint 3D horizontal and altitude trajectory control that triggers task migration.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0048","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":12,"section":"VIII. CONCLUSION"},{"report_id":"R0003","source":"P08","key":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces:1","physical_page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings"},{"report_id":"R0045","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":11,"section":"IV-B.3 and IV-C, trajectory and migration trigger"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ4","claim_id":"RQ4-B","finding":"Fault-tolerance studies distinguish wireless communication interruption from physical UAV unavailability or loss and recovery: R0063 tests Markov cloud-link flapping (2 s outage / 5 s in), AWGN BER 10^-3, and up to 30% packet loss using cognitive fallback to an autonomous-edge 7B/MAPPO stack without removing UAVs, whereas R0062 disables UAV#3's communication program to trigger partition recomputation (~2 FPS degraded, recovering to ~3 FPS on reconnect) and R0002 models random UAV failure (0.0001/slot) and battery-swap return to GCS with predictive pre-migration and recovery migration.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0063","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":16,"section":"Section 5.3.3 and Table 9, pp. 16-17"},{"report_id":"R0062","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":6,"section":"Section EFFECT OF UAV UNAVAILABILITY"},{"report_id":"R0002","source":"P08","key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","physical_page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":null}
{"rq":"RQ5","claim_id":"RQ5-A","finding":"In R0054's matched multi-seed holdout evaluation (n = 5 independent training seeds, df = 4, alpha_RF = 5, lambda_task = 0.01), GAT-HSMAO records a normalized holdout cost of 2.720 ± 0.226, lower than No-GAT (2.874 ± 0.354), Fixed-H (3.046 ± 0.297), CSI-Only (2.882 ± 0.241), and No-RIS (2.843 ± 0.288), while Fixed-RIS achieves a lower cost (2.402 ± 0.151) under the same holdout configuration; this study-specific normalized metric is not poolable across studies.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0054","source":"P08_QUANT","key":"quantity:1","physical_page":5,"section":"Table I, row 'GAT-HSMAO', column 'Norm. cost'"},{"report_id":"R0054","source":"P08","key":"partition_and_assignment:1","physical_page":3,"section":"Section II-B Three-tier DNN inference, Eq. (13) and (17e)"}],"frequency":null,"quantitative":{"report_id":"R0054","key":"quantity:1","value":"2.720 ± 0.226","units":"dimensionless (normalized cost)","baseline":"No-GAT (2.874 ± 0.354), Fixed-H (3.046 ± 0.297), CSI-Only (2.882 ± 0.241), Fixed-RIS (2.402 ± 0.151), No-RIS (2.843 ± 0.288)","denominator":"n = 5 independent training seeds (df = 4)","workload":"3,000 training episodes per seed; normal task arrival rate lambda_task = 0.01; held-out scenarios","conditions":"Matched holdout evaluation, alpha_RF = 5, Student-t 95% CI half-width (n = 5, df = 4); Fixed-RIS attains lower cost (2.402 ± 0.151)","page":5,"locator":"Table I, row 'GAT-HSMAO', column 'Norm. cost'"}}
{"rq":"RQ5","claim_id":"RQ5-B","finding":"In R0074, Orion reports 18,665 ms latency for a single 64-token Llama-2 70B FP16 request across 4 UAV edge nodes (1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G) versus Jupiter (27,264 ms), EdgeShard (43,058 ms), and Edge-Solo/Cloud-Edge (OOM); however, P09 D08 rates R0074 NO because its Introduction claims Orion is the only framework capable of running 70B on memory-constrained UAVs despite Section 4.2 showing EdgeShard and Jupiter also execute 70B, and across all 25 study units P09 D09 records 22 NOT_REPORTED (0 YES, 2 PARTIAL, 1 NO in R0089) for code/data/traces availability.","citations":[{"report_id":"R0074","source":"P08_QUANT","key":"quantity:3","physical_page":17,"section":"Section 4.2 and Fig. 4, pp. 16-17"},{"report_id":"R0074","source":"P09","key":"D08","physical_page":3,"section":"1. Introduction"},{"report_id":"R0089","source":"P09","key":"D09","physical_page":11,"section":"Data availability"}],"frequency":{"n":22,"N":25,"numerator_ids":["R0001","R0002","R0003","R0006","R0009","R0010","R0015","R0019","R0022","R0029","R0044","R0045","R0048","R0054","R0059","R0062","R0068","R0071","R0073","R0083","R0095","R0121"],"denominator":"All 25 included study units in the locked HK261 restart (appraisal_table.csv, domain D09 = NOT_REPORTED; remaining 3 are R0063 and R0074 = PARTIAL, R0089 = NO)"},"quantitative":{"report_id":"R0074","key":"quantity:3","value":"18,665","units":"ms","baseline":"Jupiter (27,264 ms); EdgeShard (43,058 ms); Edge-Solo & Cloud-Edge (OOM)","denominator":"Single 64-token request","workload":"Llama-2 70B FP16, 64-token sensor log","conditions":"1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G, 4 UAV edge nodes","page":17,"locator":"Section 4.2 and Fig. 4, pp. 16-17"}}
{"summary":true,"limitations":{"RQ1":"Architectural roles and partition granularities are heterogeneous and lack a standardized taxonomy across the 25 study units, and 8 P08 records (R0068-R0121) were truncated in the read-only viewer, preventing exhaustive P08 topology-granularity frequency counts.","RQ2":"All 25 study units are rated PARTIAL on P09 D03 (reproducible configuration), with 3 rated PARTIAL on D02 (R0001, R0022, R0062), leaving solver hyperparameters, fixed parameter boundaries, or implicit heuristics incompletely specified for exact replication.","RQ3":"Comparator validity and statistical reporting are constrained across the corpus: 24/25 study units are rated PARTIAL on P09 D04 (baseline comparability) and 23/25 PARTIAL on D06 (variability/repetitions/sensitivity), alongside 93 unretrieved full-text records and 6 records awaiting classification in prisma_counts.json.","RQ4":"Network and mobility realism is rated PARTIAL in 24/25 study units under P09 D07 (only R0054 is YES); many studies rely on static hovering or idealized channels rather than live flight-network tests, and missing failure-handling reports cannot be equated with observed absence.","RQ5":"Quantitative outcomes cannot be pooled across studies due to incompatible models, hardware, workloads, baselines, and metric definitions; claim-bound adherence is PARTIAL in 14/25 and NO in 1/25 (R0074) on P09 D08, and code/data/traces availability is 0/25 YES on D09 (22 NOT_REPORTED, 2 PARTIAL, 1 NO in R0089)."},"tools":["client:view_file","client:grep_search"],"workers_used":false,"unresolved":[{"key":"p08_extraction_table.json:tail_truncation","reason":"Read-only viewer truncated p08_extraction_table.json after R0063 (R0068, R0071, R0073, R0074, R0083, R0089, R0095, R0121 not read in full) and rationale_source_matrix.csv after R0095 D04; therefore corpus-wide P08 frequencies were omitted and exhaustive counts were restricted to fully read appraisal_table.csv (25/25) and p08_quantitative_claim_source_matrix.csv (42/42)."},{"key":"prisma_counts.json:awaiting_classification_and_not_retrieved","reason":"6 records awaiting classification (R0075, R0081, R0092, R0110, R0118, R0137) and 93 unretrieved full-text records remain outside the 25 included study units and were excluded from synthesis."}]}
